#
# The Rust notices, the licence texts under them and the policy that produces
# them are digest-bound: their SHA-256 is recorded in tools/notices/*.tsv, or
# compared between hosts by .github/workflows/rust-notices.yml, or both.
#
# Windows runners check out with core.autocrlf enabled, which rewrites every
# line ending and so changes every digest. That is not hypothetical: the first
# three-host run agreed on all three notices and disagreed on about.toml and
# pin.env, which were the only two policy files this file did not yet cover.
# pin.env is also sourced by bash, where a carriage return welded to a pinned
# version is a hazard waiting for a shell that does not ignore it.
#
# The notices and the payload additionally quote upstream licence texts
# verbatim, and some of those carry trailing whitespace. Reformatting them
# would be editing someone else's licence, so the whitespace checks are off for
# those paths rather than the text being cleaned up.
licences/rust/*.md      -text -whitespace
tools/notices/*         -text
tools/notices/texts/*   -text -whitespace

# The Rust SBOM fragments are compared byte for byte between two runs and
# between three hosts, and the CycloneDX schema files under tools/sbom/schema
# are bound by the SHA-256 recorded in tools/sbom/pin.env. A Windows checkout
# that rewrote their line endings would change every one of those answers
# without changing anything about the product.
sbom/rust/*.cdx.json    -text
tools/sbom/*            -text
tools/sbom/schema/*     -text

# The native/assets inventory is compared byte for byte between two runs of its
# generator and between three hosts, its own SHA-256 travels in the evidence
# each platform uploads, and the measured ownership maps and the two pin files
# beside it are read by a shell. A Windows checkout that rewrote their line
# endings would change every one of those answers without changing anything
# about the product.
sbom/native/*.json      -text
tools/native/*          -text
tools/occt/*            -text

# The merged product SBOMs are compared byte for byte between two runs of the
# merge and between three hosts, and each one carries the SHA-256 of the two
# documents it was merged from. A Windows checkout that rewrote a line ending
# would break every one of those answers without changing anything about the
# product.
sbom/product/*.cdx.json -text
tools/product/*         -text

# These two repository assets are hashed by the native/assets inventory and
# embedded into the viewer byte for byte. They must keep the repository bytes
# on Windows rather than being rewritten by core.autocrlf.
crates/ferritecad-viewport-gpu/src/*.wgsl -text

# The §22B-1a Unity measurement is a set of byte-compared artefacts. The two
# reader reports and the STEP transform report are compared byte for byte with
# the committed copies, every FBX fixture beside them carries a SHA-256 in
# fixture-manifest.json and PROVENANCE.json, and the generator's own output is
# compared with what is checked in. A Windows checkout that rewrote a line
# ending would change every one of those answers without changing anything
# that was measured, which is exactly what happened: the first OCCT pin run to
# reach crates/ferritecad-occt/tests/export_scene_measurement.rs found a
# report identical in every measured value and ending `\r\n` where the
# measurement had written `\n`.
tools/unity-fbx-smoke/Assets/Expected/*  -text
tools/unity-fbx-smoke/Assets/Fixtures/*  -text

# Interoperability inputs are preserved exactly as supplied. In particular,
# the C3D AP203 fixture uses CRLF throughout; treating it as source text would
# make `git diff --check` call every original line trailing whitespace and a
# Windows checkout could rewrite the regression input before its checksum is
# checked.
fixtures/step/interoperability/*.stp binary
